  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 123 PM MDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nThe upper level trough that brought the overnight/early morning\nrain and mountain snow is pushing well to our northeast, with \ndrier and more subsident airmass building in behind it. However, \nabundant low level moisture remained in place east of the Front \nRange, with the stratus deck only slowly burning off. While we do\nexpect more clearing through the late afternoon hours, areas of \nlow clouds and fog will redevelop tonight in the moist boundary \nlayer. This will be a hybrid radiational/advection fog event, \nwith contributions from a developing weak Denver Cyclone. The \nexpected location of the cyclone developing near/south of metro \nDenver and pushing slowly northeast overnight into early Tuesday \nmorning favors fog over the eastern and northern plains, but then \nalso wrapping around the back side of the cyclone down the I-25 \nCorridor into at least the northern suburbs by/shortly after \nmidnight. There's a 50-60% chance it moves as far south as the \nsouthern Denver suburbs before daybreak. Given the hybrid event \nand relatively narrow T/Td spreads, we think some areas will \nexperience dense fog. It's quite difficult to narrow the exact \nlocations of thickest fog development, but something to watch for \nthe overnight and early morning hours with potential for a Dense \nFog Advisory. We'll continue to monitor through the evening and \novernight. \n\nOn Tuesday, we're not sure that we'll see the magnitude of \nwarming we earlier anticipated. That's because of the morning \nstratus and fog, and lack of lee troughing. We've generally cut \ntemperatures a few degrees across the lower elevations but still \nmostly lower to mid 60s for highs (but a 30-40% chance we only \nhit the mid to upper 50s over the eastern plains). What we are \nmore certain about is that tomorrow will start a 3-4 day stretch \nof dry weather. \n\nThat dry weather will be courtesy of drier and brisk west to \nsouthwest flow aloft. There will be warm advection and flat \nridging by Thursday, which should support highs pushing to near \n70F across the plains and I-25 Corridor.\n\nEnsembles are still showing a subtropical push or partial remnants\n(or at least water vapor transport) of Tropical Storm Vance in \nthe eastern tropical Pacific getting drawn up as early as Friday. \nHowever, the main impacts of this would very likely (>80%) chance \nstay to the east of our forecast area. We'll only have a slight \nchance of showers reaching the far eastern plains by late Friday. \n\nA larger trough along with a possible subtropical moisture\nconnection/jet off the central and eastern Pacific reaches the \nfar southwest U.S. by late Saturday and Sunday. While that is \nfairly well agreed upon by ensemble systems, models diverge\nsignificantly by the time we reach Sunday into early next week. \nYes, there is general troughiness and storminess expected, but \nsome models move the trough through as an open or even shearing \nwave, while others develop a deep closed low somewhere between the\nMidwest and Colorado. It was interesting to note the EPS showed a\nrather marked increase in precipitation and even snow potential \nas we head toward Tuesday of next week, while the GEFS showed no \ndiscernible trend. The EPS had close to 30% of the members \nshowing snow for Denver and the plains, while GEFS supported much \nlower odds (10-20%). Some of these runs, of course, were much \ncolder than the vast majority of the ensembles, as evidenced in \nthe large temperature variations (25th-75th percentiles from highs\nin the mid 40s to mid 60s for Monday and Tuesday, but full range \nof outcomes from highs in the upper 20s to lower 70s). Stay tuned \nfor the latest trends and what could maybe, just maybe, be our \nfirst snowfall in the lower elevations should the deeper/slower \nsolutions verify.",
